  "subtitle": "The $245 hex-keypad computer that made programming feel like typing instead of flipping switches",
  "why_scout_cares": "The KIM-1 is the direct, documented answer to front-panel toggle-switch programming (the IMSAI 8080, its exact contemporary): instead of flipping switches, you type hex bytes on a 24-key keypad because a 2KB monitor in ROM called TIM greets you at power-on. It is also where the first commercially sold microcomputer game (Microchess) was born. Together with IMSAI it defines the two modes of raw machine interaction of 1976, and its successor Rockwell AIM-65 is already in the museum — completing the lineage.",
